# Gemini said
# While the square brackets [] handle the "grabbing," Python has a massive set of methods (functions attached to strings) for "transforming" and "analyzing."

# Here are the most frequently used string methods, categorized by what they actually do.

# 1. Case Conversions
# Used constantly for normalizing user input (e.g., making "Yes" and "yes" look the same).

# .lower(): Converts all characters to lowercase.

# .upper(): Converts all characters to uppercase.

# .capitalize(): Capitalizes only the first character.

# .title(): Capitalizes the first letter of every word.

# 2. Cleaning and Trimming
# Essential when dealing with user data or file reading where extra spaces might exist.

# .strip(): Removes whitespace (and newlines) from both ends.

# .lstrip() / .rstrip(): Removes whitespace from only the Left or Right side.

# .replace("old", "new"): Replaces all occurrences of a substring with another.

# 3. Searching and Positioning
# Used to find where specific text lives within a larger string.

# .find(sub): Returns the index of the first occurrence of sub. Returns -1 if not found.

# .index(sub): Same as .find(), but crashes your program with an error if not found.

# .count(sub): Returns how many times sub appears in the string.

# 4. Splitting and Joining
# These are the "Power Couple" of string manipulation.

# .split(separator): Breaks a string into a List based on the separator (default is a space).

# .join(iterable): Takes a list of strings and merges them into one string using the main string as the "glue."

# 5. Validation (The "Is" family)
# These return True or False. Perfect for if statements.

# .startswith("prefix"): Checks if the string begins with a certain value.

# .endswith("suffix"): Checks if it ends with a certain value (e.g., checking for .jpg).

# .isdigit(): Returns True if the string contains only numbers.

# .isalpha(): Returns True if the string contains only letters.

raw_data = "  johN dOE, 25, NEW YORK  "

# 1. Clean the data
clean_data = raw_data.strip() # "johN dOE, 25, NEW YORK"

# 2. Break into a list
parts = clean_data.split(", ") # ["johN dOE", "25", "NEW YORK"]

# 3. Format specific parts
name = parts[0].title() # "John Doe"
is_number = parts[1].isdigit() # True

# 4. Join back together with a hyphen
final_string = "-".join(parts) # "johN dOE-25-NEW YORK"

print(f"Name: {name}")
print(f"Age is valid: {is_number}")